WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.460
ProofMe is a record of contributions to open
projects, confirmed by the organisations that saw the work.

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:09.674
There are three roles.

00:00:10.024 --> 00:00:13.604
Contributors describe
their work and attach evidence.

00:00:13.954 --> 00:00:19.568
Organisations review those claims,
and approve, question or decline them.

00:00:19.918 --> 00:00:24.098
And anyone can check a
confirmed record, without an account.

00:00:24.447 --> 00:00:32.157
Each claim moves through clear states:
self-reported, awaiting review, verified, or not approved.

00:00:32.507 --> 00:00:39.092
Everyone you will see in this video is fictional
sample data, and ProofMe runs on NEAR testnet only.

00:00:40.436 --> 00:00:47.536
Signing up needs only a name, an email and a
password. No wallet is required to contribute.

00:00:47.886 --> 00:00:52.510
Onboarding asks for a display
name, a username and a short bio.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:01:00.765
Profiles start private. The contributor decides when
to make one public, and can hide individual sections.

00:01:03.981 --> 00:01:07.381
Here is a public profile for Daniel Okafor.

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:13.490
Verified contributions come first, each
naming the organisation that confirmed it.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:19.914
Self-reported work is shown separately, and
clearly labelled as not confirmed by anyone.

00:01:20.264 --> 00:01:25.164
Links on the profile are marked as
added by Daniel and not checked by ProofMe.

00:01:26.477 --> 00:01:29.181
Daniel signs in to add new work.

00:01:29.532 --> 00:01:36.976
A contribution has a title, a summary, a
description, the kind of work, his role and the dates.

00:01:37.326 --> 00:01:42.905
Evidence is a list of links:
pull requests, documents, demos.

00:01:43.256 --> 00:01:50.825
Every link is checked on the server. Only secure web
addresses are accepted, and nothing is fetched from them.

00:01:51.175 --> 00:01:54.665
He saves a draft, which only he can see.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:01.944
Then he chooses who should confirm it:
Northstar Protocol's Open Grants Portal project.

00:02:02.294 --> 00:02:08.019
The claim is now awaiting review. He
can withdraw it until a reviewer decides.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:16.305
Now the organisation side. Sam owns
Northstar Protocol, another fictional account.

00:02:16.654 --> 00:02:21.674
The organisation workspace shows
open claims, projects and the team.

00:02:22.024 --> 00:02:25.288
Projects group the work a claim can be about.

00:02:25.638 --> 00:02:30.903
The team has four roles:
owners, admins, reviewers and viewers.

00:02:31.253 --> 00:02:39.597
Only owners and admins invite people, and an invitation
link works once, for the invited email, for seven days.

00:02:39.947 --> 00:02:49.452
Northstar has asked ProofMe to review who runs it. That platform
review is separate from being a NEAR issuer, and the public page shows both.

00:02:50.762 --> 00:02:53.442
Priya is a reviewer at Northstar.

00:02:53.792 --> 00:02:57.276
Her queue lists the claims
sent to the organisation.

00:02:57.626 --> 00:03:01.681
She sees the full claim and the
evidence the contributor attached.

00:03:02.031 --> 00:03:08.550
She can approve it, ask for more information, or
decline it with a reason the contributor will see.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:12.175
Internal notes stay inside the organisation.

00:03:13.486 --> 00:03:15.151
She approves.

00:03:15.501 --> 00:03:26.755
At that moment the claim is frozen into an attestation: a canonical JSON
record, with a random value so it cannot be guessed, and its SHA-256 digest.

00:03:27.105 --> 00:03:33.560
A database rule stops anyone,
administrators included, from editing it afterwards.

00:03:34.863 --> 00:03:41.013
On the Attestations page, an owner connects the
NEAR account that will issue for the organisation.

00:03:41.363 --> 00:03:46.482
That account is registered as an issuer
on the ProofMe contract on NEAR testnet.

00:03:46.832 --> 00:03:59.417
Issuing an attestation mints a non-transferable NFT to the contributor, carrying only
the attestation ID, the digest and the issuer. No names, emails or links go on-chain.

00:03:59.767 --> 00:04:11.556
ProofMe never sees a key or a seed phrase. Each transaction is shown and signed in the
organisation's own wallet, and the server then confirms it from the contract before recording anything.

00:04:12.869 --> 00:04:17.129
This is the public
verification page, open to anyone.

00:04:17.479 --> 00:04:24.693
It shows who issued the record, who reviewed it, the
claim as approved, and the evidence the reviewer was shown.

00:04:25.043 --> 00:04:30.658
Every time it loads, it hashes the stored
record again and compares it with the digest.

00:04:31.008 --> 00:04:37.862
This one has not been issued on NEAR yet, and the
page says so plainly, rather than implying it has.

00:04:39.163 --> 00:04:42.028
Organisations can withdraw their word.

00:04:42.378 --> 00:04:47.397
Sam revokes an older attestation,
with a reason the contributor will see.

00:04:47.747 --> 00:04:56.297
Revoking cannot be undone. The record stays public,
marked as revoked, so nobody can quietly rewrite history.

00:04:56.647 --> 00:05:03.276
The contract also supports replacing a record with
a corrected one, which then points to its successor.

00:05:04.592 --> 00:05:10.452
ProofMe administrators review
organisations, handle reports and read the audit log.

00:05:10.802 --> 00:05:16.161
By design they cannot issue, edit or
revoke an organisation's attestations.

00:05:16.511 --> 00:05:21.291
Every permission is checked on the
server, never only in the interface.

00:05:21.641 --> 00:05:28.900
Sensitive actions are rate-limited and written to the
audit log, and the site sends a strict content security policy.

00:05:30.203 --> 00:05:36.748
Private profiles, drafts and pending claims
never appear in public search or on public pages.

00:05:37.098 --> 00:05:47.992
Current limitations: ProofMe runs on NEAR testnet only, email verification
is not switched on yet, and there is no upload of files: evidence is links.

00:05:49.305 --> 00:05:53.570
What comes next is future
work, not part of this build.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:59.189
Richer credentials, such as skills
endorsed across several organisations.

00:05:59.539 --> 00:06:06.044
And attestations about work done by AI agents,
confirmed by the people responsible for them.

00:06:06.394 --> 00:06:10.903
ProofMe: your work,
confirmed by the people who saw it.
